/**
 * The seven subjects.
 *
 * `icon` is the wire key the client resolves — it is written into the
 * category's `iconUrl` and into every one of its topics' `coverUrl` as
 * `mimo:icon/<key>`, exactly the way an avatar preset is carried. That is why
 * no payload had to learn a new field to render these.
 *
 * Stock photography is deliberately gone. It was never about the subject (the
 * Python topic wore a picture of an Arduino board), half the catalogue had no
 * photo at all and fell back to a letter in a coloured box, and a grid mixing
 * the two has no visual system holding it together.
 */
/**
 * Eighteen categories, each mapped onto a glyph the CLIENT already ships.
 *
 * `icon` is not free text: it is a key into SUBJECTS in the mobile app's
 * TopicMedallion, and that registry currently holds thirteen entries. An
 * unrecognised key does not fall back to a glyph — `resolveTopicFace` drops
 * past the scheme check and returns `{ kind: 'image', uri: 'mimo:icon/foo' }`,
 * which expo-image then tries to fetch as a URL and cannot, so the topic shows
 * an empty tile. Eighteen categories over thirteen glyphs therefore means some
 * sharing, and the pairs are chosen so the overlap reads as deliberate:
 * Economics takes the calculator, Cricket takes the trophy, English and Grammar
 * share the one language mark, Food and Drinks share the leaf, Geography takes
 * the globe. Adding `chart`, `bat`, `food`, `drink` and `map` to SUBJECTS (and
 * to Icon.jsx's GLYPHS) is a two-line change that gives each of them its own
 * mark — but it only takes effect in a build that ships, so it is left out
 * here rather than written against a client that will not have it.
 */
